nommu-bank: swap the resident pages instead of keeping one backup per process

The backend gave every process its own page set, including the one whose
memory is resident. That set is dead weight: the resident process's data is in
the region itself, and its backup is only written when it stops running. So a
region shared by N processes cost N page sets when N-1 are enough, and the
first fork of a region allocated two of them -- the parent's and the child's --
for a process whose memory had not diverged at all yet.

Exchange the resident page with the incoming shadow page instead of saving and
then restoring. After the exchange the region holds the incoming process's data
and the shadow page holds the outgoing one's, so the page set simply changes
owner; the resident owner ends up holding none. The traffic is identical: two
loads and two stores per word is what the save-then-restore pair of memcpy()
already cost. What changes is the footprint.

  program      private  backups before  backups after
  bash            276K            552K           276K
  micropython     308K            616K           308K
  make            216K            432K           216K
  dash            140K            280K           140K

bank_detach() gains the matching case: when the process leaving was the
resident one, its copy in the region is dead, so a sibling is promoted by
restoring its saved state and handing its pages back, which is the same
invariant a switch maintains.

bank_access() keeps interrupts disabled for one page rather than for the whole
request. Its length comes from __access_remote_vm() bounded only by the size of
the VMA, so reading /proc/PID/mem of a banked process could ask for a single
512 KiB memcpy with interrupts off.

The private-memory ceiling becomes a module parameter. It is also the bound on
how long a switch runs with interrupts disabled, which is the number worth
tuning against a latency budget rather than fixing at build time.


Four things were wrong with the first version of this, and all four are about
the one context this model added work to: nommu_bank_switch(), which runs
inside context_switch() with the runqueue lock held and interrupts off.

bank_move_pages() guarded itself with WARN_ON_ONCE and returned on failure.
That printk wakes the console thread, which re-enters the scheduler from
inside a context switch; the second occurrence is silent because the latch is
already set; and the early return left the contents already swapped and
region->bank_owner about to be assigned unconditionally, so one refusal
inverted the model permanently -- the new owner holding another process's
pages and the old owner holding none. The checks now happen in
bank_can_move() *before* anything is touched, a refusal leaves the region
exactly as it was, and the report is latched and printed by the next fork or
exit, which may sleep.

The "nobody owns what is resident" arm called bank_release_pages(), that is
free_page(), from inside that same context switch. The copy model's switch
does no allocation and no freeing at all, deliberately. bank_detach() now
promotes an heir on every departure so the arm should be unreachable; it
restores from its own set, keeps the pages, does not claim ownership, and
latches the report.

bank_release_pages() left freed pointers in pages[]. Harmless in the copy
model, where a descriptor is only emptied on its way out of the switch list;
in this model the resident owner sits at count 0 for its whole life, so those
slots are reachable from a live descriptor. They are cleared with the page.

And bank_clone() now refuses a second bank for a region the target mm already
banks. Two of them would make nommu_bank_switch() walk that region twice in
one pass: the second visit swaps it back, leaving the process on a stale
snapshot with its live pages parked in a descriptor that no longer owns them.
dup_mmap()'s vm_usage check happens to prevent it today, but that check is
there for other reasons.
